{"chunk_id":"R2-1802623-001","collection":"ran2_tdoc_chunks","metadata":{"source":["MediaTek"],"tdocNumber":"R2-1802623","title":"Early Contention Resolution and RRC Resume"},"point_id":"2581431723222318936"}
This document is spawned from discussion of R5 test case by ref [1]. The document contains two parts, one relating to Early Contention Resolution and one relating to RRC Resume, which may seem unrelated but came together in discussions on the test case in question. R5 discussions has been about the following sequence: The UE performs RACH for Resume. It has been unclear for the Resume case, whether the stored physical layer configuration is actually resumed or not, and when it is resumed. It has been unclear whether the DL RRC Resume message can be received by CSS or USS or both. This relates also to the R2 discussion on Early contention resolution. On early Contention Resolution, we believe the current status is correct: According to TS 36.213, the UE monitors CSS during the RACH procedure, and the RACH procedure ends at contention resolution. According to TS 36.213, the UE monitors USS when the UE has a USS configuration. Thus, the UE behavior for the case when a USS configuration is provided AFTER the RACH procedure is not very strictly specified in 36.213 for NB-IoT. However, for LTE/MTC it is specified that the UE shall continue to monitor CSS until a USS configuration is received, and such behavior could be considered reasonable for NB-IoT as well. In 36.331, regarding the physical layer configuration including the USS configuration, it is actually not clear whether it is resumed or not. However, we believe that if the physical layer configuration is resumed it can only be used as a baseline for the new configuration when the DL RRC resume message is received, and before this, the default Physical layer and default MAC-Main configurations are used.

{"chunk_id":"R2-2010994-004","collection":"ran2_tdoc_chunks","metadata":{"source":["Qualcomm"],"tdocNumber":"R2-2010994","title":"Power saving enhancements for paging reception"},"point_id":"7766865190462107768"}
g early indication is not studied in Rel-17. Proposal 2. If PDCCH-based solution(s) for paging early indication is to be studied, leveraging existing DCI format(s) should be prioritized. Observation 1. Sending a DCI-based indication before a PO (PDCCH-WUS) does not save more power than using legacy DCI with cross-slot scheduling (CSS-P), if UE is not paged. Observation 2. UE consumes extra power to receive a page if PDCCH-WUS is used, unless PDCCH-WUS also schedules paging PDSCH. Observation 3. Further studies are needed on how network may choose a good gap between a PDCCH-WUS and its associated PO that is power efficient for all UEs. Observation 4. CSS-P has less spec impact and are easier to implement than PDCCH-WUS. Proposal 3. Prioritize studies on CSS-P over PDCCH-WUS. Observation 5. UE grouping by multiple P-RNTI is more flexible and can support more UE groups than other schemes. Observation 6. Having multiple P-RNTIs only creates very low blocking or overhead on paging channel. Observation 7. If necessary, network can configure P-RNTIs and UE behavior such that at most one paging DCI is sent even if multiple legacy and/or new UEs in the same PO are paged. Proposal 4. Using multiple P-RNTIs is considered for UE grouping.

